  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1118 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening showed a shortwave trough \nsliding across northeast NE into western IA with an upper level \nridge axis just to our west. Said shortwave along with warm air \nadvection and some weak frontogenesis aloft was leading to \nshowers and a few storms across the eastern Sandhills. These \nwill slowly continue eastward, though have shown a diminishing \ntrend as they have done so owing to less instability this way, \nso not expecting them to amount to much in our area. However, \nadditional development is expected overnight as moisture \ntransport ramps up with latest short term guidance in decent \nagreement of at least some spotty showers lingering across \nnortheast NE into the afternoon. A few solutions even show those\nshowers extending as far south as the KS border. As it stands, \nhave 30-40% chances in far northeast NE and 20% chances \nextending south from there, but diminishing as they push east \ntoward Lincoln and Omaha. In general, still expect much more dry\ntime than wet during the day Wednesday as that aforementioned \nridge axis pushes east through the area.\n\nOn its heels, though, will be a steady stream of shortwave \nenergy out of the Desert Southwest, warm air advection, and \nmoisture transport that will lead to persistent rain chances \nlasting into Saturday. Chances across most of the area never dip\nbelow 40% in that period and generally peak around 80-90% in \nthe overnight/morning hours. With limited instability, not \nexpecting much in the way of thunderstorms, though with \nprecipitable water values in the 1.50-1.75+ inch range \n(approaching the 99th percentile of climatology for this time of\nyear) there could be some periods of very efficient rain \nproduction. Totals through that period look to be in the 1-2\" \nrange for most of the area. Temperatures also look to remain on \nthe cooler side, with highs in the 60s to around 70 through \nFriday. Assuming precip tapers off by Saturday afternoon, we \nshould see some mid to upper 70s return to the area. Upper level\nridging will build in on Sunday and lead to a dry and warmer \nday with a few spots in southeast NE and southwest IA making a \nrun at 80 degrees.\n\nHeading into early next week, there is good agreement that \nshower and storm chances will return, but there remains quite a \nbit of spread in timing of those chances. The general idea is \nthat the upper level ridge pushes off to the east with southerly\nflow allowing a good plume of gulf moisture to push into the \narea. In addition, some guidance hints that some tropical \nmoisture out of the Pacific on the edges of Hurricane Polo could\nwork its way toward the area. Eventually, a potent shortwave \ntrough pushes straight east out of CA, spinning up a surface low\neast of the Rockies, with the system eventually moving through \nthe forecast area and giving us widespread shower and storm \nchances. Again, timing of this wave is still up in the air, but \nit looks to be sometime Monday or Tuesday, with maybe a slight \ntrend toward being later. Given the potential tropical moisture \nplume in place with a little instability and the expected \nrainfall a few days prior further saturating the ground, a \nflooding threat could develop. In addition, given the strength \nof the system, there will likely be some decent shear in place, \nwhich could yield a severe thunderstorm threat. For what it's \nworth, machine learning severe weather guidance is starting to \nsuggest at least a 5% chance of severe storms by Monday. Would \nexpect that to increase a bit somewhere in the region as \nensemble guidance comes into better agreement on system timing. \nOverall, still a lot of details to work out, but early next week\nwill be a time period to keep an eye on.",
